 # Security
 
+Public overview on the live site: `/security`. Account controls: Settings → Security / Sessions.
+
 ## Trust boundaries
 
 - **Platform control plane** (Next.js app, Postgres, Redis): trusted operators only.
 - **Candidate workspaces**: untrusted source, uploads, and AI-generated instructions.
 - **Cybersecurity labs**: intentionally vulnerable apps confined to the sandbox; never external targets.
+- **Guest paths**: `/learn`, `/challenges`, `/jobs` (and `/security` docs) without sign-in; mutations still require auth + CSRF.
 
 ## Execution isolation
 
@@ -16,13 +19,21 @@
 
 ## Application controls
 
-- Argon-strength bcrypt password hashes (cost 12).
-- HTTP-only session cookies, SameSite=Lax.
-- RBAC on every mutating API.
-- Rate limiting via Redis.
+- bcrypt password hashes (`BCRYPT_ROUNDS`, default 10 for serverless latency).
+- HTTP-only session cookies, SameSite=Lax; password change revokes other sessions.
+- Origin CSRF on mutating `/api/*` (middleware); Stripe webhook exempt.
+- Public hosts refuse `DEMO_SKIP_EMAIL_VERIFY` / `ALLOW_DEMO_ACCOUNTS`.
+- RBAC on mutating APIs; Redis (or in-memory) rate limits — Jobs refresh is tightly capped.
 - PDF résumé magic-byte + size validation.
 - Audit log for auth, assignment, execution, review, incident actions.
 - AI keys only via environment variables / provider `apiKeyEnv` indirection.
+- Lesson bodies render markdown as React nodes (no `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`).
+
+## Gaps / ops notes
+
+- No self-serve password-reset email flow yet — rotate via Settings while signed in, or admin assist.
+- CSP still allows `'unsafe-inline'` scripts until full nonce plumbing with Next.js.
+- Session tokens are stored opaque in Postgres (not hashed at rest).
 
 ## Assessment ethics
 
